1\. What information was visible in the Telnet TCP stream? Commands, usernames, and other data were visible as readable plaintext.

2\. What changed when you followed the SSH TCP stream? The session data was encrypted and no longer readable as ordinary plaintext.

3\. Why is sending authentication information over an unencrypted session dangerous? Attackers can intercept and read usernames and passwords.

4\. What security property does SSH provide that Telnet does not? SSH provides encryption to protect credentials and session data.

5\. Why should packet captures containing credentials never be uploaded to a public GitHub repository? Anyone could access the credentials and use them to compromise accounts or systems.
